commit 8754f782af3a8b1bef70af54956ae03d334f8d9e

C03: length-cut drafts are unpublishable, «-enen» is not a stutter, «investiga X» and «qué pasó hoy» search (WEB1451)

WEB1449 measured, on one weather variant, a draft that the 256-token composition budget cut mid-sentence («…pero no contienen») and that the mind published anyway; the App then rejected it as internal_code because ContainsStutteredToken took the ordinary verb ending «-enen» for a stutter. The category's remaining open rows also showed two request shapes nobody read as public lookups.

baxy_mind llm: a compose candidate whose generation finished by length is remembered and rejected (cut_by_length) at every stage, with a hint asking for a report of at most three sentences and three items that finishes its last sentence.
Baxy.App UserMessagePolicy: ContainsStutteredToken counts only a doubled tail of three or more letters; a doubled two-letter tail is ordinary morphology («contienen», «intereses», «preparar», «succeeded»), and the measured stutters («llamarar») stay listed by token.
baxy_mind effect_intent: «investiga / investigá / research X» (optionally «sobre», «acerca de», «en internet») is a public lookup of the topic X (_topic_research_query; not for questions, files or notes), and «qué pasó / qué está pasando hoy (en el mundo)» is a news lookup.
baxy_mind __main__: the search query for those forms is the topic itself («Spider-Man») or «noticias de» plus the person's scope words («noticias de hoy en el mundo»; «news today in the world»), because the engine answers neither the research verb nor the question; the news word is the reader's and is exempt from literal grounding.

Offline probes: the three literals and six variants of the next panel resolve to one web.search with the shaped query; the boundaries («¿Qué es un buscador?», «No investigues nada en internet.», «¿Qué significa la palabra noticia?») resolve to nothing. App recompiled (BUILD1451). Tests not run (owner order).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>


